WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.400
For the next few minutes, we'll dig into putting hooks to work in real engineering scenarios.

2
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.800
We'll walk through six patterns, starting with auto-formatting on file right

3
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.439
and blocking destructive bash commands, then logging tool calls for audit trails,

4
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.959
notifications on task completion, automatic test runs after edits, and API cost tracking.

5
00:00:22.959 --> 00:00:29.200
Each pattern maps to a specific hook type. You finish a refactor and the pull request comes

6
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.319
back covered in comments about indentation. Nobody meant to skip the formatter, they just

7
00:00:34.319 --> 00:00:39.680
forgot. That's exactly the gap hooks close, and a few of them do most of the work.

8
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:46.080
An auto-format hook is a post-tool use hook that runs a code formatter after any file is written,

9
00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:52.319
so style is never a manual step. A safety net hook is a pre-tool use hook that inspects pending

10
00:00:52.319 --> 00:00:58.240
bash commands for dangerous patterns and stops them before they execute. An audit log hook is a

11
00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:04.080
post-tool use hook that records every tool call with a timestamp, leaving a traceable history of

12
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:10.959
what the session actually did. Which one you reach for follows from timing. The auto-format pattern

13
00:01:10.959 --> 00:01:16.080
is one of the most common hook use cases. Developers forget to run formatters manually,

14
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:21.599
and inconsistent style creates noise in code reviews and slows down pull request cycles.

15
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:27.519
The solution is a post-tool use hook that fires after any write or edit call,

16
00:01:27.519 --> 00:01:33.919
then immediately runs black, prettier, or whatever formatter the project requires. Post-tool use is

17
00:01:33.919 --> 00:01:40.239
the write hook type here because the file already exists on disk when the hook fires. The formatter

18
00:01:40.239 --> 00:01:46.559
edits the saved file and the result is style compliant code on every write with zero extra

19
00:01:46.559 --> 00:01:52.239
effort from the developer or from Claude. It's one of the fastest wins a team can get from hooks.

20
00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:59.919
The safety net pattern uses pre-tool use because pre-tool use fires before the command executes.

21
00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:05.599
That timing is everything. The hook inspects the incoming bash command string and checks it

22
00:02:05.599 --> 00:02:12.800
against a list of dangerous patterns like rm-rf, drop table, format disk, and similar destructive

23
00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:19.440
operations. If a match is found, the hook returns exit code 2, and Claude's execution stops

24
00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:26.000
immediately. Exit code 2 is the one that blocks. Exit code 1 and other non-zero codes are treated

25
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:33.039
as non-blocking errors, so the command still runs. A post-tool use hook can't help here because post-tool

26
00:02:33.039 --> 00:02:38.880
use fires after the action is already done. Prevention requires pre-tool use. Knowing which

27
00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:44.639
hook type to reach for based on timing is one of the most important hook concepts to internalize.

28
00:02:45.839 --> 00:02:50.639
Audit logging has several implementation flavors depending on what the team needs.

29
00:02:50.639 --> 00:02:57.600
A simple append-only log writes the tool name, arguments, and timestamp as plain text after each

30
00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:04.000
tool call. It's easy to implement and readable without tooling. A structured JSON log stores

31
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.880
each entry as a JSON object, which makes it easy to filter and query programmatically.

32
00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:15.360
Remote logging pushes entries to an external endpoint for centralized storage across many

33
00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.880
sessions, which is useful in team environments. A stop hook adds a final summary entry when the

34
00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:26.960
agent finishes its run, giving the log a clean end marker. The right format depends on whether

35
00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:31.919
the log is consumed by humans reading a terminal or by machines running queries.

36
00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:38.559
It's worth being clear about when pre-tool use versus post-tool use is the right choice for

37
00:03:38.559 --> 00:03:45.279
logging. Pre-tool use logging records intent before execution. It captures what was attempted,

38
00:03:45.279 --> 00:03:51.039
including actions that were subsequently blocked. That's useful for detecting policy violations

39
00:03:51.039 --> 00:03:57.440
because a blocked action still leaves a record in a pre-tool use log. Post-tool use logging records

40
00:03:57.839 --> 00:04:03.759
results after execution. It captures what actually succeeded, including output and

41
00:04:03.759 --> 00:04:10.160
side effects. If you need a record of completed work, use post-tool use. If you need to catch

42
00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:15.759
attempted violations, pre-tool use gives you visibility into blocked actions as well as

43
00:04:15.759 --> 00:04:21.519
completed ones. Many teams run both hooks together to get a complete picture of agent

44
00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:27.760
behavior from intent all the way through execution. Notification hooks use the stop

45
00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:32.799
lifecycle event. Stop fires once when the agent reaches the end of its execution loop,

46
00:04:32.799 --> 00:04:38.480
which makes it a natural trigger for completion signals. A typical implementation calls a slack

47
00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:44.799
webhook, sends a system notification, or posts to a monitoring endpoint. The payload should include

48
00:04:44.799 --> 00:04:50.959
the task name, elapsed time, and whether the session ended cleanly or with an error. This

49
00:04:50.959 --> 00:04:56.000
pattern is most useful for long-running batch jobs, where the developer steps away and needs

50
00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:01.359
to know when results are ready without pulling the terminal. It decouples the developer's attention

51
00:05:01.359 --> 00:05:07.040
from Claude's execution time, which is especially valuable on tasks that take 10 or 20 minutes to

52
00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:13.279
complete. Test run hooks close the feedback loop between writing code and confirming it works.

53
00:05:13.839 --> 00:05:19.679
The hook fires post-tool use on write or edit calls, then checks the file path to decide whether

54
00:05:19.679 --> 00:05:25.839
to run tests. You don't want the full test suite to execute every time Claude edits a documentation

55
00:05:25.839 --> 00:05:32.640
file or a configuration comment. Scoping the hook to test relevant paths, like anything under SRC or

56
00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:40.079
lib, limits execution to meaningful changes. When the path matches, the hook runs Pytest, Jest, or

57
00:05:40.079 --> 00:05:46.720
whatever runner the project uses via a bash call. The developer sees test results immediately after

58
00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:52.880
each code edit, with no context switch required. That tight feedback loop makes it much harder for

59
00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:59.839
a broken test to slip through unnoticed. Cost tracking hooks monitor API usage at the session

60
00:05:59.839 --> 00:06:06.079
level. A post-tool use hook parses usage from the tool and transcript output where it's available,

61
00:06:06.079 --> 00:06:10.959
recording the model name, a timestamp, and any token counts the payload exposes.

62
00:06:11.519 --> 00:06:16.959
It appends that record to a session cost file or pushes it to a local database for aggregation,

63
00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:22.079
reviewing the data across a session, surfaces which prompts are expensive,

64
00:06:22.079 --> 00:06:28.079
and whether overall usage is trending toward budget limits. Teams running automated pipelines

65
00:06:28.079 --> 00:06:33.519
use this pattern to catch runaway sessions before they generate unexpected invoices.

66
00:06:33.519 --> 00:06:40.160
It's a lightweight addition that pays off quickly on high-volume workflows. Without it, cost overruns

67
00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:44.320
often go unnoticed until the billing cycle ends and the damage is already done.

68
00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:51.679
Hooks allow you to attach your own deterministic behavior to fixed points in the agent's life cycle

69
00:06:51.679 --> 00:06:57.279
and can be very powerful. It matters because formatting, test runs, and audit trails stop

70
00:06:57.279 --> 00:07:02.720
depending on someone remembering to do them. One thing to watch, the event you pick decides whether

71
00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:08.880
you're observing or intervening. Post-tool use can only report on what already happened.

72
00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:14.799
Stopping something requires pre-tool use. That does it for this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

